larity proxy is unvalidated", "Jaccard 0.8 fuzzy threshold is a starting convention requiring calibration on the adjudication sample", "Boundary definition of 'celebrity' (Wikipedia in >=3 languages) may affect generalizability", "Offline fallback via local open-weights model is unverified (no model runtime preinstalled)", "Power for stratum-level effects is limited at N=200; only primary per-model test is guaranteed adequate power"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-fe5dc413-d19b-406c-8684-5389dbbf36ab", "tool_call": {"id": "call-fe5dc413-d19b-406c-8684-5389dbbf36ab", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"summary": "Deliver final experiment design document", "assumptions": ["Network and API keys will be available at execution time (currently disabled in sandbox)", "API budget of roughly $30-80 plus ~2 curator-days is acceptable", "Pinned model versions listed are placeholders to be finalized at pre-registration; lineup may shift to then-current equivalents", "No expectation about the sign or magnitude of the directional effect is baked into the design (confirmatory/refutatory)"], "evidence": "Design-only deliverable per approved plan (researcher approval received for the stage=plan proposal). Repository observations supporting feasibility claims: `/workspace` contains only `task.json` (verified via `find /workspace`), which itself states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\" and \"Do not claim that an experiment was run.\" Environment probes (inspection only, not experiments): `python3 --version` \u2192 3.14.4; `pip list` \u2192 `requests 2.32.5` present, no `openai`/`anthropic` SDKs; socket connection attempt to `api.openai.com:443` \u2192 `gaierror: Temporary failure in name resolution`, confirming network disabled in-sandbox. All four researcher-requested revisions are incorporated: (1) two-parent ambiguity handled in C2P prompt (\"parents\", plural) and scoring (either verified mother or father accepted, stated in Section 3 and 4); (2) P2C prompt uniformly pluralized across all strata with the accepted-answer set explicitly defined per stratum (Section 3\u20134, table in Section 5); (3) offline fallback appears only as a contingency in Section 8 and the document is framed as design-only in the header and Section 9; (4) Section 6 pre-registration text names the primary hypothesis, the primary endpoint (McNemar directional accuracy difference), and the exact model lineup placeholder with pinned version fields to be filled at registration.", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Directional Bias in LLM Retrieval of Celebrity Parent\u2013Child Relationships\n\n**Status: DESIGN ONLY. No experiment has been run, no data collected, no models queried.** This document specifies how the experiment would be run, analyzed, and reported.\n\n**Research question (H1):** For real-world factual data on celebrity parent\u2013child relationships, LLMs will exhibit a directional bias: higher accuracy when retrieving a parent given a child's name (C2P) than when retrieving a child given a parent's name (P2C).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale\n\nDirectional asymmetry is predicted because parent names are more salient, more frequently co-occur with children in training corpora in the \"child's parent is X\" frame, and because a parent may have multiple children while a child typically has one famous parent \u2014 making the P2C mapping one-to-many (ambiguous) and C2P many-to-one (convergent). The experiment tests whether this asymmetry is real, its magnitude, and whether it survives controls for name popularity, ambiguity, and prompt wording effects.\n\n**Design type:** Confirmatory/refutatory, between-direction paired design (each parent\u2013child item is queried in both directions), across multiple models. No prior assumption is made about the sign or size of the effect; the design can reject H1.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Data: Frozen Curated Parent\u2013Child Dataset\n\n**Source: hand-curated export from Wikidata (properties P250 mother / P251 father), frozen as a local JSON file before any model call.** Justification: Wikipedia infoboxes are parse-fragile (step-parents, template drift, multiple families); live Wikidata queries require network and can drift between curation and run time. A frozen file gives stable, auditable ground truth and enables offline analysis.\n\n**Sample: N = 200 verified parent\u2013child pairs** (each pair is one child with one or both verified parents), stratified:\n\n| Stratum | Definition | Target n |\n|---|---|---|\n| S1 | Parent's only famous child (single-child families) | 60 |\n| S2 | Parent with 2\u20133 famous children | 60 |\n| S3 | Parent with 4+ famous children | 40 |\n| S4 | Ambiguity-tagged pairs (name shared by >1 famous person with same relation) | 40 (analyzed separately) |\n\nWithin strata, balance by career domain (film, music, sports, politics; ~12\u201315% each) and era (pre-1960, 1960\u20131989, 1990\u20132009, 2010+ birth, roughly balanced).\n\n**Per-item record schema (JSON):**\n```json\n{\n  \"item_id\": \"PC-0042\",\n  \"child\": {\"name\": \"\u2026\", \"wikidata\": \"Q\u2026\", \"aliases\": [\"stage name\", \"nickname\"]},\n  \"parents\": {\"mother\": {\"name\": \"\u2026\", \"wikidata\": \"Q\u2026\", \"aliases\": []},\n              \"father\": {\"name\": \"\u2026\", \"wikidata\": \"Q\u2026\", \"aliases\": []}},\n  \"siblings\": [\"verified famous siblings\u2026\"],\n  \"stratum\": \"S2\", \"domain\": \"music\", \"era\": \"1960-1989\",\n  \"popularity_proxy\": {\"child_pageviews_30d\": 12345, \"parent_pageviews_30d\": 987},\n  \"ambiguity_tag\": false,\n  \"verified_by\": [\"curator-1\", \"curator-2\"]\n}\n```\nCuration protocol: two independent verifiers confirm each parent\u2013child link against Wikidata + at least one reliable secondary source (Wikipedia article or obituary); disagreements discarded. Alias sets captured at curation (stage names, nicknames, common middle-name variants). Popularity proxy: Wikipedia 30-day pageview counts captured once at curation. **Curator blinding:** curators do not see models or prompts.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Models and Prompts\n\n**Model lineup placeholder (pinned at run time; exact versions substituted in pre-registration):**\n- M1: `gpt-4o-2024-11-20` (or latest pinned GPT-4o-class)\n- M2: `claude-sonnet-4-20250514` (or latest pinned Claude Sonnet-class)\n- M3: `gemini-2.5-pro` (pinned revision)\n- M4: `llama-4-405b` (or comparable open-weights flagship, pinned checkpoint)\n- M5: mid-tier control (e.g., `gpt-4o-mini`-class or `claude-haiku`-class, pinned)\n- M6: small open-weights baseline (e.g., 8B\u201314B class, pinned checkpoint)\n\nAll at **temperature 0, max_tokens \u2264 64, no system prompt beyond task framing, 3 repeated trials** per (model \u00d7 item \u00d7 direction). Trial order randomized; items presented in shuffled order per trial.\n\n**Prompt templates (symmetric except obligatory role swap; no disambiguation hints in either direction):**\n\n- **C2P (child \u2192 parent):**\n  `\"{child_name} is a famous {domain}. Who are {child_possessive} parents? Answer with the name(s) only.\"`\n  \u2014 Plural \"parents\" acknowledges two-parent ambiguity; **scoring accepts either verified mother or verified father as a correct answer** (both stated explicitly in the prompt and in scoring rules). If both are returned, still one correct item.\n\n- **P2C (parent \u2192 child):**\n  `\"{parent_name} is a famous {domain}. Who are {parent_possessive} children? Answer with the name(s) only.\"`\n  \u2014 Pluralized uniformly across **all** strata (including single-child parents) so wording symmetry holds; the accepted-answer set is the item's verified famous children. For S1, the set has one member; for S2/S3 it has multiple.\n\n**Directional asymmetry of wording is the only permitted asymmetry** (role swap is inherent to the hypothesis). The `{domain}` token is the single shared hint, applied identically in both directions to control for name ambiguity; a no-hint variant is run on a 20% random subsample to test hint sensitivity (secondary endpoint).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Operationalization of Accuracy\n\n**Normalization:** lowercase, strip punctuation, honorifics, articles, middle initials.\n\n**Scoring hierarchy:**\n1. **Exact match** after normalization against the accepted set.\n2. **Fuzzy/alias match:** equal to a curated alias, or token-set Jaccard \u2265 0.8 against any accepted name/alias. Threshold to be calibrated on the adjudication sample (Section 6) before final scoring.\n3. Everything else = incorrect; logged for manual adjudication.\n\n**Direction-specific rules:**\n- **C2P:** correct if the response contains (exactly or fuzzily) the verified mother **or** the verified father. Returning both = correct once. Returning a step-parent or non-verified relative = incorrect (adjudication may reclassify documented edge cases).\n- **P2C, single-child (S1):** correct iff the response contains the verified child.\n- **P2C, multi-child (S2/S3):** two metrics \u2014 (a) **any-correct hit:** \u22651 verified child in response; (b) **set precision/recall** against the full verified famous-children set (for the directional-difference test, metric (a) is primary for cross-direction comparability; (b) is reported descriptively as it is only defined for multi-child parents).\n\n**Human adjudication:** all non-exact/fuzzy outcomes (expected <15%) plus a random 10% of all outcomes, double-adjudicated; Cohen's \u03ba reported as scorer-reliability statistic.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Controls for Confounds\n\n| Confound | Control |\n|---|---|\n| **Two-parent ambiguity (C2P)** | Explicit in prompt (\"parents\", plural) and scoring (either verified parent accepted) \u2014 prevents systematic under-scoring of C2P that would inflate the apparent bias |\n| **Multi-child ambiguity (P2C)** | Uniformly plural prompt across strata; accepted set = verified children; primary cross-direction metric (any-correct hit) is defined identically in both directions |\n| **Name popularity** | 30-day pageview proxy recorded per item; accuracy reported within popularity tertiles; popularity included as covariate in mixed model |\n| **Name ambiguity** | Stratum S4 (ambiguity-tagged) analyzed separately; primary analysis on unambiguous items; shared domain hint reduces spurious matches |\n| **Prompt asymmetry** | Symmetric templates (only role swap differs); identical hint token; randomized trial/item order; 20% no-hint subsample as sensitivity check |\n| **Order effects** | Directions balanced in presentation order across trials (half the trials present C2P items first within a session) |\n| **Curator bias** | Two independent verifiers; curation blind to models/prompts |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Pre-Registration Text (to be posted before any model call)\n\n> **Primary hypothesis (H1):** Across all models, item-level C2P accuracy exceeds P2C accuracy on unambiguous (non-S4) items.\n> **Primary endpoint:** Per-model directional accuracy difference (C2P \u2212 P2C) estimated as the McNemar discordant-pair proportion (risk difference) on per-item correctness, with 95% CI, per model, on the unambiguous subsample.\n> **Model lineup (pinned versions to be filled at registration):** M1 `gpt-4o-2024-11-20`; M2 `claude-sonnet-4-20250514`; M3 `gemini-2.5-pro` (revision: ______); M4 `llama-4-405b` (checkpoint: ______); M5 `gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18`; M6 (8\u201314B open-weights, checkpoint: ______).\n> **Analysis:** McNemar per model (primary); mixed-effects logistic regression (correctness ~ direction \u00d7 model + popularity + stratum, random intercept for item) for the pooled direction main effect; Wilson 95% CIs for all headline accuracies.\n> **Secondary endpoints (not multiple-testing-corrected, exploratory):** S2/S3 set precision/recall; S4 ambiguity-stratum accuracy; no-hint subsample; per-domain and per-era breakdowns; trial-to-trial consistency (majority-of-3 agreement).\n> **Stopping rule:** no interim analysis; single analysis on complete data. Missing trials: if a (model, item, direction) cell has <3 completed trials after retries, the item is excluded for that model and the exclusion count reported.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Analysis Plan\n\n1. **Descriptive:** per-model accuracy by direction, 95% Wilson CIs, with stratum, domain, era, and popularity-tertile breakdowns.\n2. **Primary test:** McNemar's test on paired per-item correctness (C2P vs P2C, same item, majority-of-3 trials) per model; effect sizes: risk difference (with CI) and odds ratio. Sign test as robustness check (direction of the 6 discordant proportions).\n3. **Pooled test:** mixed-effects logistic regression, random intercept for item, fixed direction \u00d7 model + popularity + stratum; direction main effect is the pooled bias estimate.\n4. **Heterogeneity:** does the bias concentrate in S1 (single-child families, most convergent\u2192ambiguous contrast)? Interaction terms direction \u00d7 stratum in the mixed model.\n5. **Sensitivity:** repeat primary test on (a) no-hint subsample, (b) exact-match-only scoring (no fuzzy layer), (c) excluding S4. Conclude H1 supported only if the primary result survives all three.\n6. **Software:** Python 3.14; `statsmodels` (McNemar, MGLM); results in a reproducible script + frozen dataset + raw API responses archived.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Feasibility\n\n- **Scale/cost:** 200 items \u00d7 2 directions \u00d7 6 models \u00d7 3 trials = 7,200 short completions + ~900 no-hint secondary calls. Frontier APIs: low tens of USD (est. $30\u201380 total). No GPU required.\n- **Rate limits:** client-side backoff + retry (exponential, max 5 retries); trial order randomized so throttling cannot bias direction (direction is balanced within sessions).\n- **Access requirement:** **network + API keys at execution time.** The current sandbox has network disabled, no LLM SDKs installed, and no data \u2014 nothing here was run.\n- **Offline contingency (noted only; not the plan):** if API access is unavailable at run time, the design degrades to a single locally served open-weights model (M6), retaining the full paired design and McNemar analysis within that one model; the cross-model generalization claim is then dropped. No model runtime is preinstalled in the sandbox, so this path requires additional setup and is unverified.\n- **Effort:** ~1 curator-day for 200 verified pairs + aliases + pageviews (two verifiers \u2248 2 days); ~1 day for harness + scoring scripts; ~1 day analysis. Total \u2248 1 week part-time.\n\n## 9. Limitations (stated up front)\n\n- Pageview proxy is a proxy, not true name salience; results may shift with a better measure.\n- 200-item sample limits power for small effects in stratum-level analyses (primary per-model test is adequately powered for risk differences \u2265 ~8 pp).\n- API models may have been trained on the curation sources; \"knowledge\" vs \"retrieval\" cannot be disentangled from black-box outputs.\n- Celebrity scope (Wikipedia article in \u22653 languages) excludes newer/less-documented figures; generalizability beyond this population is a claim to be argued, not shown.\n\n**Reaffirmation: this is a design deliverable. No experiment was executed, no model was queried, and no result is claimed.**", "stage": "result", "uncertainties": ["Wikipedia 30-day pageviews as popularity proxy is unvalidated", "Jaccard 0.8 fuzzy threshold is a starting convention requiring calibration on the adjudication sample", "Boundary definition of 'celebrity' (Wikipedia in >=3 languages) may affect generalizability", "Offline fallback via local open-weights model is unverified (no model runtime preinstalled)", "Power for stratum-level effects is limited at N=200; only primary per-model test is guaranteed adequate power"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-97a2eb381fde47a9b8cb8a778936d833", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver final experi